  "summary_en": "Oregon Revised Statutes chapter 837 (2025 edition) governs aircraft registration and operation and includes a dedicated unmanned aircraft (drone) regime: limits on law-enforcement drone use, public-body registration and data policies, bans on weaponized drones, critical-infrastructure and interference offenses, civil remedies, and state preemption of local drone laws.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Law enforcement may use drones only with a warrant (max 30 days), consent, emergencies, search and rescue, or crime scene reconstruction; unlawfully acquired evidence is inadmissible.",
   "Public bodies must register drones with the Oregon Department of Aviation, file annual use reports and publish data retention policies.",
   "Operating weaponized drones is a misdemeanor or felony, with exceptions requiring FAA authorization, notice and $1M liability insurance.",
   "Offenses for flying over critical infrastructure, harassing over private premises, and interfering with aircraft or emergency response.",
   "State preempts local drone regulation, except takeoff/landing rules in local parks; property owners may sue for trespass with treble damages."
  ],
